{"version":2,"kind":"Article","sha256":"e9b7ef7375e135992770abbcdc5f9fd790f7b39c2d0da0ae994acae8f60f9a2f","slug":"valentinianism","location":"/gospel/gnosticism/valentinianism.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Valentinianism","authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Eric Araujo","given":"Eric","family":"Araujo"},"name":"Eric Araujo","corresponding":true,"email":"eric.araujo@calvin.edu","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/Flavia_Sophe_inscrip-cc23926a340e8ee0a156e43508e67fc6.jpg","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"valentinianism.md","url":"/valentinianism-509612c6906aa23ec38877af87e82c08.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valentinianism was one of the major Gnostic Christian movements in the second century, founded by Valentinus (c. 100–160 CE), a prominent Gnostic teacher. Valentinus taught a complex cosmology that involved a series of divine emanations (Aeons) from a supreme, unknowable God. According to Valentinian belief, the material world was created by a lesser deity, often identified with the God of the Old Testament, who was ignorant of the higher spiritual realities.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"YFatGMORjL"}],"label":"valentinianism","identifier":"valentinianism","html_id":"valentinianism","key":"cORvhsLxjF"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valentinians believed that human beings contained a divine spark or seed that originated from the higher spiritual realm. This divine spark was trapped within the material body, and salvation involved awakening this spark through gnosis (knowledge) of one’s true spiritual nature and origin. Valentinian teachings emphasized the role of Jesus Christ as a revealer of this secret knowledge, who came to impart gnosis to those capable of receiving it.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"iyWo7peBCX"}],"key":"s0qy38plRu"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valentinianism also had a distinctive view of the church and sacraments. Valentinians often practiced a form of initiation that involved baptism and a special rite known as the “bridal chamber,” which symbolized the union of the soul with its divine origin.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"WtFazNg5uf"}],"key":"bWqStuGOqF"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/Flavia_Sophe_inscrip-cc23926a340e8ee0a156e43508e67fc6.jpg","alt":"Valentinian inscription from the grave stele of Flavia Sophe, referencing the bridal chamber. Source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"KEsQgasztD","urlSource":"../figures/Flavia_Sophe_inscription.jpg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valentinian inscription from the grave stele of Flavia Sophe, referencing the bridal chamber. ","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"H6DYQ1EDYM"},{"type":"link","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentinianism","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"ZLY79Lbz21"}],"urlSource":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentinianism","data":{"page":"Valentinianism","wiki":"https://en.wikipedia.org/","lang":"en"},"internal":false,"protocol":"wiki","key":"kLKl54mUJ6"}],"key":"H2229vk9AG"}],"key":"MHtOT5S4vx"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"kyOX9pn6qz"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Pleroma","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"GB9JEMUUxI"}],"identifier":"the-pleroma","label":"The Pleroma","html_id":"the-pleroma","implicit":true,"key":"e4mJjxSs2S"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Central to ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"u7M5jXoPDo"},{"type":"crossReference","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valentinian","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"elCfAXcpJ5"}],"identifier":"valentinianism","label":"valentinianism","kind":"paragraph","template":"Paragraph","resolved":true,"html_id":"valentinianism","key":"M1CcanOGod"},{"type":"text","value":" cosmology is the concept of the ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"dhwoZ9yzYj"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pleroma","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"McmBlJkSjb"}],"key":"F8kY0rk5NQ"},{"type":"text","value":", the fullness of the divine realm inhabited by the Aeons. The Pleroma is a complex hierarchy of divine beings, each representing different aspects of the divine nature. At the top of this hierarchy is the ultimate God, from whom all Aeons emanate in pairs, reflecting a balance of masculine and feminine principles.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"IbXfbniIjv"}],"label":"pleroma","identifier":"pleroma","html_id":"pleroma","key":"sNySmERYpe"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":